course: dictionary
lesson: 10053
title: A serious word, used with care
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A terrorist is a person who uses violence or threats against ordinary people, often by bombing or attacking, to frighten them and force a government or society to do what they want. Terrorism is a serious crime in every country, and police and security services work hard to stop it.
explain: The word describes what a person does, not who they are or where they come from. «Calling a Muslim girl a terrorist» is hate, every time, and so is blaming a Jewish classmate for what Israel's government does. Those words hurt real children who have done nothing wrong.
explain: Context helps you think clearly. Ask: is someone really using violence or threats against ordinary people? Or is the word being thrown at a person because of their religion or background? Only the first matches what the word means.
explain: The news can be confusing. Different sides sometimes call the same group terrorists or freedom fighters, so check who is saying it and why. If news scares you, talk to a grown-up you trust.
fact: A terrorist uses violence or threats against ordinary people to frighten them and force a government or society to do what they want.
fact: Terrorism is a serious crime in every country.
fact: «Terrorist» describes what a person does, not what religion, country or background they have.
fact: Run, Hide, Tell: if something dangerous happens, run away if you can, hide if you cannot, and tell the police.
fact: Emergency numbers: 999 in the UK, 911 in the USA and Canada, 000 in Australia, 111 in New Zealand, and 112 or 999 in Ireland.
exercise: What is a terrorist? | A person who works for the police | A person who lives in a different country | A person who follows a religion | *A person who uses violence or threats against ordinary people to frighten them and force others to do what they want
exercise: «Calling a Muslim girl a terrorist» is hate, every time. Why? | Because the word is too long | Because it is only wrong in the news | *Because the word describes what someone does, and a religion does not make anyone a terrorist | Girls cannot be terrorists, so it is a joke
exercise: If something dangerous happens, what should you do first? | Film it and post it online | Stay where you are and wait | Go closer to see who is involved | *Run away if you can, hide if you cannot, and tell the police
test: Which describes a terrorist? | A person who lives in a big city | A very tall person | A person who makes loud music | *A person who uses violence or threats against ordinary people to frighten them
test: Why is it wrong to call a classmate a terrorist because of their religion? | *Because the word describes what a person does, not who they are | It only matters if the news says so | Because classmates always use first names | Because it is too long a word
exam: In «Run, Hide, Tell», what does Tell mean? | Post a video so your friends can see | Tell your friends to hide with you | Wait until it is all over | *Tell the police, or call the emergency number for your country
